From 2bc039b9bfb07df0bc20d45e59316a84ab50d8f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:19:21 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] perf(sidebar): one poll reads each store once [daveey]

Replay of GET /api/profiles/sessions/sidebar every 2 s, live ~/.hermes: 167-176 -> 79-86 ms CPU per poll
(Desktop's own query: 85-116 -> 56-63), same responses. One poll in three passes the 5 s response cache, and
each of those spent ~600 ms:

- The recents, cron and messaging slices each annotated through _all_counts; a board or hub.db write between
  slices recomputed every count (~130 ms) up to three times. one_annotation_snapshot() makes the slices share one
  read of the counts, waiting sources and attention counts (a failed read is retried, never shared).
- Each board was opened five times per recompute (~6 ms even when empty). The reads now share the board's cached
  read-only connection and one read transaction, and are memoized per board on its db/-wal stat; the stall
  verdict is re-judged against the clock on every use.
- list_sessions_rich(order_by_last_active) evaluated the session filter and every session's MAX(message
  timestamp) twice. The page is now picked from chain_max (exactly the admitted roots) and columns/last_active
  are projected onto the page only: same rows, 35-50% less CPU per list on the live 12 GB state.db.
- The pinned back-fill is driven from the pinned ids (it walked every session through the filter, ~14 ms per
  slice, for a handful of pins).
- The replica read memo compared last_ok, which moves on every hub pull (~2 s): it compares the stale verdict and
  each card's synced_at instead (memo hits 1/28 -> 35/43 live reads, every hit equal to a forced full read).
- The remote projection resolves session tips on one state.db connection (was ~24 opens of the 12 GB file), and
  every _remote_links reader inside one_annotation_snapshot shares one projection.
- remote_links_and_origin_rows() exposes the ledger project() rows behind the cached remote projection, so
  chat-closeout no longer re-projects the ledger on every pass.

